 Cross-chain wallet state cannot be expressed as a native EVM predicate.  Wallet state on non-EVM chains (Solana, XRPL, Bitcoin) can’t be evaluated from a Solidity predicate. The natural shape is for an off-chain issuer to evaluate the condition set against the relevant chain data, sign a verdict, and have the on-chain predicate verify the signature. The spec’s  data  parameter (“Opaque context bytes (e.g., tokenId, proof,  signature )”) is designed for exactly this. 


 It’s distinct from the  AccessProof  pattern in §“Account Parameter Is Advisory.”  That pattern is requester-self-signed — the wallet signs a challenge to prove it is  account . The attestation pattern here is issuer-signed — an external service signs a verdict about  account ’s wallet state. Identity-binding and state-binding are orthogonal; a complete access scheme may want both, and conflating them at the marker layer would lose that distinction. 


 Gas fits comfortably.  ECDSA P-256 verification via the RIP-7212 precompile is ~3,450 gas; ABI decode of a seven-tuple proof is another few thousand. Well under the 200k cap. 


